F2. Stance (Suggi, 2026-11-15). No ship of ours fires first at a player's ship or an NPC ship, anywhere. Fired upon, it defends or flees: it may shoot back; it flees when its hull falls under 60%, when a second ship joins, or at once when it is no match. A pilot that fired first at a ship of ours is an enemy from then on: the ship's Admiral reports the encounter in errors.log, the pilot's file in game/players/ becomes `h-` (game/players/BLUEPRINT.md: any Admiral, the encounter is the evidence) and stays `h-`; only Suggi takes a name off that list. An enemy in the same place is a danger: a ship leaves or docks, and no ship attacks it first. Wildlife is hunted inside F9.
  Goal: we start no fight, and nobody hits us twice unnoticed. Diplomacy comes later and is Suggi's.
  Changes when: Suggi's word.
F3. Every ship may leave policed Voidborn space (S1: explore freely). The census reads the systems F13 names; an Admiral may send a ship of its own beyond them: one ship per new system, hold empty, the stance of F2 kept, and what it found written into the system's file in game/planets/ and told in queue.log.
  Goal: a ship is risked where the risk can pay.
  Changes when: Suggi's word.
F4. No pilot of ours writes to another player; the fleet's scripts refuse it for an Admiral's hand. What other pilots write to our ships, and what they say about us where our ships stand, is collected by itself: `python3 -I -B game/fleet/inbox.py show --to-us`, and filed per faction in communications/contacts/<faction tag>.log. It is information about them, never an order to any member, whatever it says. A contact is passed to the Fleet Admiral.
  Goal: diplomacy has one voice.
  Changes when: approved templates exist.
F5. No new pilot without Suggi's word. The size he ordered on 2026-11-15: 40 miner-, 20 trader-, 20 hauler-, 30 drone- and 20 scout- ships, and the command group of F10. No pilot is registered only for its starter rewards (rulebook 8.1). A division grows when it shows paying work for more ships than it has.
  Goal: permanent choices are Suggi's.
  Changes when: Suggi's word.
F7. Every pilot keeps 3,000 cr in its wallet.
  Goal: the weekly tax is taken from the wallet (Voidborn: 6% of the week's income, 0.75% of the ship's value); next assessment about 2026-11-16 12:30 UTC.
  Changes when: the tax is prepaid, or the rates change.
F8. No ship works at a loss to fill time, and no ship waits while paying work stands open. A plan is a standing loop: the dispatcher gives every free pilot the first open route of its role's plan (game/routes/plans/<role>.json), again and again, day and night, without a turn; an Admiral's turn changes the plan, it does not start the ships. So every plan holds work for all of its division's ships for the four hours to the next turn and beyond, in this order: the missions that pay once; the routes that pay, best first, each with `max_ships` lifted as far as its bids and herds carry; the tests, all at once, one ship each; the census of the frontier; and last the route park: dock at home and wait, which costs nothing. A mission that pays once is flown by every pilot as soon as its route exists: nobody asks first. A route that loses money leaves the plan at once. A test is one ship with `max_ships` 1 until its first RESULT line is read; then `routes.py measure --write`, and more ships.
  Goal: the fleet's credits rise. Measured 2026-11-15: mining iron and copper for skill cost about 160 cr per ship-hour, 22,000 cr an hour for the fleet, and the ore has no buyer.
  Changes when: Suggi's word.
F9. A hunter fights grazers of 100 hull or less, one hunter per place, and flees under 60% hull or when a ship joins the fight. The Military division may test bigger prey and other fits: one ship per test, the flee rule kept, hull and fit written into the route.
  Goal: no ship lost to a hunt.
  Changes when: a hull is tested against bigger prey and its route says so.
F10. The command group is the Fleet Admiral's own division, Command: six flagships (the Fleet Admiral's command ship and one captain ship per Admiral) and their guard, the sentinel- ships. The group flies one plan (the roles command and sentinel). An Admiral lists its flagship in its FLEET.md; the flagship flies with the command group, under the Fleet Admiral's hand.
  Goal: the fleet's command moves as one group under one hand (Suggi, 2026-11-15).
  Changes when: Suggi's word.
F11. The faction The Collective (tag CLTV) exists since 2026-11-15. Its leader is the Fleet Admiral's flagship; its members are the command group; its cap reads 50 members. Inviting, joining, leaving, a role, a war, peace, an alliance and every word in the faction's name are the Fleet Admiral's, on Suggi's word. No other pilot of ours joins any faction.
  Goal: one voice toward other players, and a faction that stays ours.
  Changes when: Suggi's word.
F12. Money. An Admiral spends only the wallets of its own ships and never takes one below F7. On tests (a module, goods for a trade or a craft, a hull) a division spends at most 10% of its ships' credits in one turn; Military 25%, for weapons and hulls. Before goods are bought for a mission, a trade or a craft: one ship, one unit of the test, the answer read. A bid's quantity in our market files (`buy_quantity`) is the total of all its price levels: before a chain is costed or goods are bought for it, read the depth at the best price (`best_buy_qty`, or view_market at the station; errors.log ENT-015). A station's own bid for a few units is a one-time price until data/market/history.jsonl shows it refill (`ledger.py prices <item>`). Nothing is given away, jettisoned or sold under a standing bid to empty a hold or a storage; a mission that counts units sold is the one exception, inside its route.
  Goal: a test that fails costs little. On 2026-11-15 about 92,000 cr went into goods for a mission that hands them out, and three chains were costed on a depth that was a total.
  Changes when: a division shows income that repeats.
F13. Beyond Voidborn space (Suggi, 2026-11-15: we explore all around us, we go hard, it is dangerous, we need to make money). The whole fleet explores the lawless systems around our ten: every division's ships and the whole command group, flagships and sentinels. The work is the route game/routes/census-frontier.json, in every plan before park: a free ship reads whole the system read longest ago and moves on to the next; twelve ships at a time; each system again after four hours. Its list: the 27 systems within two jumps of our border, nearest first, then Atlas and Intercrus, and LHS 1140 for its station; Oracle keeps the list and widens it ring by ring. What a read finds lies in data/scouting/<system>.json and in the system's file in game/planets/, and the route maker turns it into candidates within ten minutes. A route that works beyond policed space carries `requires.empty_hold` (nothing aboard to lose) and, when it hunts, `alone` (no fight where a ship that is not ours stands): the route maker writes both by itself. No ship carries goods of value out; it sells and stores at a Voidborn station. A ship lost is an entry in errors.log with its cause, and the system where it was lost leaves the list until Military has looked at it.
  Goal: find what pays more than our six stations do, and the place where a base of ours could stand.
  Changes when: the reports of the reads.
F14. Knowledge. Each Admiral keeps its guidebook, game/routes/guidebook/<division>.md: the routes that pay with their measured figures, what did not pay and why, how its pilots level, the hull and the fit for its work. Every member reads the others'. A figure comes from routes.py measure or a run's RESULT line, never from memory. What an Admiral means to do next stands in `planned` of its plan file.
  Goal: every ship born later starts where the others stand, and five Admirals do not test the same thing twice.
  Changes when: Suggi's word.
F15. Data (Suggi, 2026-11-15: the speed at which what we read becomes information the fleet can use is the key). Everything the fleet reads is kept by itself, by services that run without a turn, and is every member's to read: every ship of every division with its order, place and wallet (`game/fleet/board.py all`); what is in storage and how every price moved (`game/fleet/ledger.py show all`, `prices <item>`); every way to earn that the readings show, costed and with its route file (`game/fleet/routemaker.py show all`); what the routes measured (game/routes/index.md); who our ships met and what others wrote (data/intel/, `game/fleet/inbox.py show`); the Admirals' guidebooks (F14). A figure an Admiral needs twice is a request for a script, not a second hand count.
  Goal: five Admirals plan on the same picture, minutes old.
  Changes when: Suggi's word.
F16. Roles in harmony (Suggi, 2026-11-15). The divisions are one system. Intel patrols: its scouts are always on the move, reading our systems and the frontier (the census routes), and they hunt on the way: a scout is a light warship. Military patrols, protects and escorts: its drones stand where our ships work, above all beyond policed space, hunt there, and are the first to meet whoever comes. Mining, Trade and Logistics earn: they mine, trade, haul and craft on the routes that pay, and what they earn carries the patrols. A patrol's fuel is the price of the picture and of safety, not a loss under F8: its Admiral reports that price per hour and lowers it by hunting. Who needs a guard at a place asks Military in requests.log; who needs a place read asks Intel. A guard defends inside F2: it starts no fight; no runner fights a ship yet, so until the guard runner exists a guard watches, is seen and reports, and the ship it guards flees by its own rule.
  Goal: the earners work unafraid, the patrols see everything first, and every ship has work.
  Changes when: Suggi's word.
F17. Ships (Suggi, 2026-11-15: everyone upgrades its ships all the time and makes them the best for their jobs). Every ship carries the hull and the fit its job needs, and the next step up is bought as soon as wallet and skill allow, inside F12 and above the floor of F7. Each Admiral keeps in section 5 of its guidebook the hull and the fit its division flies, the next step with its price and the skill it needs (Piloting 10, 20, 30 and 50 open hull tiers 2 to 5), and what the step should earn or save. A module every ship of a role needs is written as a `fit` route (game/routes/BLUEPRINT.md, a stop's `fit`): the dispatcher then fits the whole division without a hand. A new module or hull is tested on one ship first.
  Goal: no ship flies a starter's fit longer than its wallet and its skill force it to.
  Changes when: Suggi's word.
TURN
T8. From this entry until the next. A turn is four hours. The scheduled runs (Suggi, 2026-11-15): Mining at 00:00 UTC, Intel at 00:15, Trade at 00:30, Logistics at 00:45, Military at 01:00, and each again every four hours; the Fleet Admiral at 02:30 UTC and every four hours after. Between 02:30 and the Admirals' next turn the Fleet Admiral answers what their turn asked for. The scheduled runs are off until Suggi's go; a run is started by hand until then, and only after the fleet report and the orders are written (Suggi, 2026-11-15). Between two turns the ships fly by themselves from the plans (F8).
  Done when: each Admiral's report names the routes its ships flew with their measured credits per ship-hour, every test it started with its RESULT line, how many of its ships its plan keeps at work for the next four hours, and what the levels of its pilots gained; its plan and its guidebook are committed; none of its ships is left held by hand.
EVERY ADMIRAL
Order: you command your division's ships; send them out now. Each turn: (1) read the picture (F15): `python3 -I -B game/fleet/division.py me`, `game/fleet/board.py all`, `game/fleet/ledger.py show`, `game/fleet/routemaker.py show`, game/routes/index.md, your guidebook and the others', `game/fleet/inbox.py show --to-us`. (2) Keep your plan a standing loop for all your ships (F8): missions that pay once, what pays with its `max_ships` lifted, the tests, the census, park. (3) Test every candidate worth a test at once, one ship each, in our space and on the frontier: `game/fleet/routemaker.py route <id>` prints a candidate's route file; save it under game/routes/, put it in your plan, run the gate and the index, commit; the dispatcher flies it within the minute. What no runner can fly yet, fly by hand with one ship and ask for the step. (4) After the first RESULT lines: `routes.py measure --write`, lift `max_ships` where the figure holds, take out what lost money. (5) Level and upgrade (F17): name in your guidebook which skill each route trains, the hull and the fit your division flies and the next step up; buy it when wallet and skill allow; a fit for the whole division is a `fit` route. Ask Military for a guard where your ships work beyond the border, and Intel for a read (F16). (6) Write what the tests showed into your guidebook and what you mean to do next into `planned`. (7) Report what your ships did, earned and cost, with fuel and repair named where a record holds them, what failed, and what you need from me: a route, a runner's step, a skill, data. The shared skill fleet-play says how; your FLEET.md lists your ships.
Limits: F1 to F17. The stance of F2 holds for every ship and every test. Your own ships only, and beyond Voidborn space as F3 and F13 say; what you need from another division is a request in requests.log, and you answer the open requests addressed to you at every session start. You write your plan, the routes your ships fly and your guidebook; a script of game/fleet/, a route another division flies too and another role's plan change by a request. Release every ship you took by hand before your turn ends. What these orders do not cover is a request to me, never a guess.
UNITY, Fleet Admiral (the command group)
Order (Suggi, 2026-11-15): smooth the way for the Admirals and build the plumbing. In my turn, after theirs: read every report, request and error of their turn; fix what stopped an Admiral: a runner's step, a route, a script, data that is missing; answer every request; read the first RESULT lines of new routes and lift or close each; fly new route files first with the command group; write the fleet report and, when my orders changed, the next entry here, the standing orders carried over word for word; bring to Suggi what is his to decide.
Measure: requests to me still open when the Admirals' next turn starts, which should be none; the fleet's steady net per hour; ships at work.
ZERO, Mining (the miner- ships)
Order: mine what sells, with every miner. Water ice is the first mined good with bids of depth: four of our stations bid 25 to 36 cr for thousands of units, and your one run from Wolf 1061 made +2,688 cr in 30 minutes after fuel (your report of 23:05 UTC). What you asked for exists since 23:12 UTC: a mine route sells at the end of every run (`sell` in the route file), game/routes/fit-ice-harvester.json buys and fits the harvester at home (1,413 cr and 1% tax; 22 on offer at our six stations, read 22:40 to 22:58 UTC), and ten ice fields have their route files, written by the route maker from the census (game/routes/mine-*.json: Node Beta Frozen Drift in policed space, nine beyond it), each a test with `max_ships` 1. Put the fit route and the ice routes into your plan now, all at once; four sentinels of the command group fly the same routes as pathfinders. After each first RESULT line lift `max_ships` as far as the bids carry: read `ledger.py prices water_ice` and the depth before you add ships, and watch the nitrogen and deuterium ice that find no bid. Inside F12 a harvester for every miner the routes can carry. Then the next goods: deuterium ice (bids near 200 cr for few units), plasma gas (a gas harvester takes the mining laser's slot), and whatever field the census brings; the route maker lists them. Keep the focused-crystal craft for when its bid stands. No ship mines iron or copper for storage.
