ORACLE, Intel (the scout- ships)
Order: Patrol and hunt (F16). Your scouts are always on the move: set `every_minutes` and `max_ships` of the two census routes so that a scout that is not hunting is reading, our ten systems first and then the frontier, and report what the patrol costs per hour. Between reads your scouts hunt: put hunt routes into the scout plan, share the herds with Military by request so that one hunter stands at one place, and fly the test hunts nobody has flown: `routemaker.py show --kind hunt` lists every herd of prey of 100 hull or less and says where what a kill yields is not known; one scout per species, inside F9, the loot sold and written down. Name the fit a scout needs to hunt and to get away (F17). lead the reading of the frontier (F13) and keep the picture fresh. game/routes/census-frontier.json is yours: the list, how often, how many ships at once. It holds 30 systems now; widen it ring by ring toward the nearest stations of other factions, and take a system off where a ship was lost. Every read becomes candidates in the route maker within ten minutes: say in queue.log what a read found that no script can see. For each system say whether a base could stand there (lawless, a place without a station; GOAL). Watch who is in our space: the larger groups of one faction per system are on the command station's map and in data/station/; the faction files are in game/factions/. Read `game/fleet/inbox.py show` at every turn: what other pilots write near our ships is yours to judge, and a message to us is the Fleet Admiral's to answer. File every pilot that fired first at a ship of ours as `h-` in the same turn (F2). Keep every Voidborn system read within the hour. The corrections of game/rules/ for releases 0.715.3 to 0.717.0 are still owed to you (requests.log ENT-038).
Found 2026-11-15: 18 frontier systems read by 22:04 UTC, none with a station; every home system read within the hour; the factions most seen in our space in 24 hours were OLSV, IRLC, HIVE and BUSY (the public jump stream, 22:23 UTC), and our own ships had met pilots of each of them by 19:07 UTC, the first reading of the service that records it.
